+## Trusted helper, sealing, user verification (0.2.2)
+
+No credential is ever written to a plain file by this crate (rightkit#771 D4): keys live in the OS store or in memory, and `secret_from_env_file` only reads a file an operator provisioned.
+
+- `AppleHelper::new_trusted(path, HelperTrust)` / `with_trust`: the path is canonicalized and must be a regular file inside the app bundle, and on macOS signed by the required Team ID (default: the host app's own; `TeamPolicy::Fixed(id)` or `Skip` for development). Checked before every spawn; the canonical path is what runs. `apple_helper::resolve_bundled_login_item(name)` finds `Contents/Library/LoginItems/<name>.app/Contents/MacOS/<name>`; `LoginItemLookup::new().dev_fallback(true)` adds a bare-sibling lookup for an unpackaged executable.
+- `Sealer` + `seal_blob`/`open_blob`: AES-256-GCM under a key held in the credential store, or Windows DPAPI. `PinSealKey`: an HMAC seal key kept as one credential-store item, wrapped under the owner's PIN once one is set. `wrap_key`/`unwrap_key`/`derive_pin_key`: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256, 310,000 rounds.
+- `PartitionPolicy` with `verify_login_keychain_item`/`read_login_keychain_item` (macOS): trust a login-keychain item only if its partition list is exactly your team's. Defence in depth, not a boundary; seal the value too.
+- `verify_user` (Windows Hello, `windows-hello` feature) and `verify_p256`/`owner_key_id` (verify an owner-key approval). macOS Touch ID and the Secure Enclave owner key are in RightKitSwift. A TPM-backed signing key is not provided.
+- Written without a compiler run; the OS-facing parts (DPAPI, Windows Hello, the keychain partition read, the macOS signature check) are unverified on Windows and macOS hardware (see the 0.2.2 changelog entry).
+
